--TEST--
`Core\Request\Part::readAll`'s bound is one `uint` of bytes in a closed bag, so a size written as text is refused and so is an option the bag does not declare — while `content()` takes nothing at all — `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bag, asked at both of its edges. `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways` writes the deliberate
// large buffer as `max: "200M"` in prose; the argument is a count of bytes,
// because a size in text would be a second parser in front of a number and
// `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused` refuses to guess what one means. `[limits] memory` is written that
// way in `nvs.toml` and is the configuration's spelling, not the program's.
//
// Beside it the closed half: an option the bag does not declare is refused
// rather than ignored, so a misspelt bound cannot silently leave the call at
// its default -- which is exactly the failure that would let a program believe
// it had raised a limit it had not.
//
// And `content()` is the member with no bound to state at all: it holds one
// chunk whatever the upload is, so there is nothing for a caller to say.
foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
    tainted bytes $sized = $part->readAll({max: "200M"});
    tainted bytes $misspelt = $part->readAll({limit: 200});
    $part->content(1024);
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `uint`, found `string`
   --> case.nvs:16:49
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
